### Insertion at the Tail of a Linked List

**Problem:** Given the head of a singly linked list and an integer `X`, insert a new node with value `X` at the end (tail) of the linked list and return the head of the modified linked list.

**Approach:**

- Create a new node with the given value `X`.
- Check if the linked list is empty (`head == null`). If so, return the new node as the head.
- Initialize a temporary pointer with the head node.
- Traverse the linked list until reaching the last node (`temp.next == null`).
- Connect the last node to the newly created node (`temp.next = newNode`).
- The new node becomes the last node of the linked list.
- Return the original head.

**Time Complexity:** O(n) – In the worst case, we traverse the entire linked list to reach the last node.

**Space Complexity:** O(1) – Only one new node and a temporary pointer are used.

**Concepts Used:**
- Singly Linked List
- Node Insertion
- Tail Pointer
- Temporary Pointer
- Node Linking
- Reference Manipulation
- Null Checking

###Solution

class Solution {
    public ListNode insertAtTail(ListNode head, int X) {
        ListNode newNode = new ListNode(X);
        
        // If the linked list is empty, return the new node as the head
        if (head == null) {
            return newNode;
        }
        
        // Traverse to the last node
        ListNode curr = head;
        while (curr.next != null) {
            curr = curr.next;
        }
        
        // Attach the new node at the tail
        curr.next = newNode;
        
        return head;
    }
}
